parsyl — atomic primitives
==========================

Token-shaped parsers and char-level building blocks. Lives alongside
`parsyl/parsyl.lsysl` under the same `module parsyl`.

Surface in this file:

  - `accept_if(label, pred)` — single-rune predicate matcher.
  - `literal(s)` — string-prefix matcher.
  - `regex(pattern)` / `r(pattern)` / `string.r` — POSIX regex,
    anchored at the cursor (Pike-VM via `std.regex`).
  - `digit` / `letter` / `alphanum` — conventional char predicates.
  - `chr(c)` / `one_of(s)` / `none_of(s)` — char-set primitives.
  - `take_while(pred)` — consume run-of-runes, return as string.
  - `chars_to_str(cs)` — join a char-slice through `StrBuilder`.
  - `int_p` / `hex_digit` — small numeric scanners.

Everything here returns a `Parser[X]`; composition with operators
(`~`, `|`, `~>`, `<~`, `^^`, `^^^`, `!`, `&`) is provided by the
trait machinery in `parsyl/operators.lsysl`.

    module parsyl

    import std.bytes.{as_bytes, has_prefix}
    import std.utf8.{decode_rune}
    import std.builder.{new_builder}
    import std.regex.*
    import std.debug.{assert}
    import std.strconv.{parse_int}
    import std.result.{unwrap}


accept_if
---------

Read one rune if it satisfies the predicate. `label` is woven into the
failure message — pass something the user can act on like `"digit"` or
`"alpha"`.

    accept_if(label: string, pred: (char) -> bool) -> Parser[char] =
        Parser[char]((inp: Input) ->
            if inp.at_end() then
                Failure(s"expected ${label}, got end of input", inp)
            else
                val (c, n) = decode_rune(inp.source[inp.offset : len(inp.source)])
                if pred(c) then Success(c, inp.advance(n))
                else Failure(s"expected ${label}", inp))


literal
-------

Match a literal string prefix. Uses `std.bytes.has_prefix` over the
unconsumed slice — empty string matches at every position.

    literal(s: string) -> Parser[string] =
        val sb = as_bytes(s)
        Parser[string]((inp: Input) ->
            val rest = inp.source[inp.offset : len(inp.source)]
            if has_prefix(rest, sb) then
                Success(s, inp.advance(len(sb)))
            else
                Failure(s"expected literal '${s}'", inp))


regex
-----

Match a POSIX regular expression at the current cursor position. The
pattern is parsed and compiled to bytecode once, at parser construction;
the returned `Parser[string]` reuses the same bytecode on every call.

Anchoring: the regex must match starting at the current cursor — even
if the pattern itself is unanchored. We achieve this by running the
engine on the unconsumed slice and rejecting any match whose start
offset is nonzero. (POSIX leftmost-longest semantics still apply
within the match that starts at zero.) Use `^...` / `...$` inside the
pattern only to constrain start-of-source / end-of-source if you want
those.

The Scala parser-combinator analogue is
`regex(r: Regex): Parser[String]` from `RegexParsers`. Wrap with
`lex(...)` for the typical "skip whitespace, then match" form.

    regex(pattern: string) -> Parser[string] =
        val (root, nodes, classes, n_groups, err, msg, _) = parse_regex(pattern)
        assert(!err, s"invalid regex /${pattern}/: ${msg}")
        val insts = compile(root, nodes, n_groups)
        Parser[string]((inp: Input) ->
            val rest = string(inp.source[inp.offset : len(inp.source)])
            val m = exec(insts, classes, rest, n_groups)
            if m.matched() && m.starts[0] == 0 then
                val n = m.ends[0]
                Success(string(inp.source[inp.offset : inp.offset + n]), inp.advance(n))
            else
                Failure(s"expected match for /${pattern}/", inp))

`r(pattern)` is a short-name free-function alias for `regex(pattern)`,
useful when the pattern lives in a variable: `r(my_pat) ~ "=" ~ r(my_pat)`.

    r(pattern: string) -> Parser[string] = regex(pattern)

`string.r` is the extension-method form, producing the *exact* Scala
parser-combinator surface — `"\\d+".r ~ "=" ~ "[a-z]+".r`. Parameterless
extensions take no parens, so the call site reads identically to
Scala's postfix-`.r` on `StringOps`. The extension is brought into
scope by `import parsyl.*` (or any selective import that pulls in this
function).

    extension (s: string)
        r -> Parser[string] = regex(s)


Char-level atoms
----------------

`digit`, `letter`, and `alphanum` are conventional char-level
combinators that every parser library ships under these names. They
stay as building blocks for users composing their own char-level
parsers; for full-token work the regex extension (`"[0-9]+".r`,
`"[A-Za-z]+".r`, etc.) is usually a better fit.

    digit -> Parser[char] =
        accept_if("digit", (c: char) -> '0' <= c <= '9')

    letter -> Parser[char] =
        accept_if("letter", (c: char) -> ('a' <= c <= 'z') || ('A' <= c <= 'Z'))

    alphanum -> Parser[char] =
        letter | digit


Char-set primitives
-------------------

`chr(c)` matches one specific character.

    chr(c: char) -> Parser[char] =
        accept_if(s"character '${c}'", (x: char) -> x == c)

`one_of(chars)` matches any one character from the given string;
`none_of(chars)` matches any one character that is *not* in the given
string. Both decode `chars` as UTF-8, so multibyte runes work too.

    one_of(chars: string) -> Parser[char] =
        val cs = as_bytes(chars)
        accept_if(s"one of '${chars}'", (c: char) ->
            var i = 0
            var found = false
            while i < len(cs) && !found do
                val (rn, n) = decode_rune(cs[i : len(cs)])
                if rn == c then found = true
                i = i + n
            found)

    none_of(chars: string) -> Parser[char] =
        val cs = as_bytes(chars)
        accept_if(s"none of '${chars}'", (c: char) ->
            var i = 0
            var ok = true
            while i < len(cs) && ok do
                val (rn, n) = decode_rune(cs[i : len(cs)])
                if rn == c then ok = false
                i = i + n
            ok)


take_while
----------

`take_while(pred)` consumes runes while the predicate holds, returning
the matched span as a `string`. Always succeeds — the empty string is
a valid result. Useful for scanning identifier-like or whitespace-like
runs without the `rep(accept_if(...))` + `chars_to_str` ceremony.

    take_while(pred: (char) -> bool) -> Parser[string] =
        Parser[string]((inp: Input) ->
            var i = inp.offset
            var done = false
            while i < len(inp.source) && !done do
                val (c, n) = decode_rune(inp.source[i : len(inp.source)])
                if !pred(c) then done = true
                else i = i + n
            Success(string(inp.source[inp.offset : i]), Input(inp.source, i)))


Numeric scanners
----------------

`chars_to_str(cs)` builds a string from a char slice via `StrBuilder`.
Useful when a parser accumulates `[]char` and the caller wants a
`string`.

    chars_to_str(cs: []char) -> string =
        val b = new_builder()
        for i in 0..<len(cs) do
            b.write_rune(cs[i])
        b.to_str()

`int_p` parses an optional sign followed by one or more decimal
digits, with surrounding whitespace handled by `lex`. The regex
matches `std.strconv.parse_int`'s grammar exactly, so the conversion
is just a fold over a known-valid string.

    int_p -> Parser[int] =
        lex("-?[0-9]+".r) ^^ ((s: string) -> unwrap(parse_int(s)))

`hex_digit` matches a single hex digit (0-9, a-f, A-F) and returns
the corresponding 0..15 value.

    hex_digit -> Parser[int] =
        accept_if("hex digit", (c: char) ->
            ('0' <= c <= '9') || ('a' <= c <= 'f') || ('A' <= c <= 'F')) ^^
            ((c: char) ->
                if '0' <= c <= '9' then int(c) - int('0')
                else if 'a' <= c <= 'f' then int(c) - int('a') + 10
                else int(c) - int('A') + 10)
Common-atoms tests
------------------

    #test
    test_digit_accepts() -> unit
        digit()(Input(as_bytes("3"), 0)) match
            Success(c, n) ->
                if c != '3' then panic(s"expected '3', got '${c}'")
                expect(n.offset, 1, "digit advances by 1")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_digit_rejects() -> unit
        digit()(Input(as_bytes("x"), 0)) match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("digit should reject 'x'")
            Failure(_, n) -> expect(n.offset, 0, "digit does not advance on failure")

    #test
    test_letter_accepts_lowercase() -> unit
        letter()(Input(as_bytes("k"), 0)) match
            Success(c, _) -> if c != 'k' then panic(s"got '${c}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_letter_accepts_uppercase() -> unit
        letter()(Input(as_bytes("Q"), 0)) match
            Success(c, _) -> if c != 'Q' then panic(s"got '${c}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_letter_rejects_digit() -> unit
        letter()(Input(as_bytes("4"), 0)) match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("letter should reject digit")
            Failure(_, _) -> ()

    #test
    test_alphanum_accepts_letter() -> unit
        alphanum()(Input(as_bytes("w"), 0)) match
            Success(c, _) -> if c != 'w' then panic(s"got '${c}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_alphanum_accepts_digit() -> unit
        alphanum()(Input(as_bytes("0"), 0)) match
            Success(c, _) -> if c != '0' then panic(s"got '${c}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_alphanum_rejects_punct() -> unit
        alphanum()(Input(as_bytes("!"), 0)) match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("alphanum should reject '!'")
            Failure(_, _) -> ()

    #test
    test_chars_to_str() -> unit
        if chars_to_str(['h', 'i', '!']) != "hi!" then panic("chars_to_str failed")

    #test
    test_int_p_unsigned() -> unit
        parse_all[int](int_p(), "42") match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, 42, "int_p 42")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_int_p_signed() -> unit
        parse_all[int](int_p(), "-17") match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, -17, "int_p -17")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_int_p_with_ws() -> unit
        parse_all[int](int_p(), "   123   ") match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, 123, "int_p strips ws")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_int_p_rejects_garbage() -> unit
        parse_all[int](int_p(), "abc") match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("int_p should reject 'abc'")
            Failure(_, _) -> ()

    #test
    test_hex_digit_lowercase() -> unit
        val p = hex_digit()
        p(Input(as_bytes("a"), 0)) match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, 10, "hex_digit 'a' = 10")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_hex_digit_uppercase() -> unit
        val p = hex_digit()
        p(Input(as_bytes("F"), 0)) match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, 15, "hex_digit 'F' = 15")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_hex_digit_decimal() -> unit
        val p = hex_digit()
        p(Input(as_bytes("7"), 0)) match
            Success(v, _) -> expect(v, 7, "hex_digit '7' = 7")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_hex_digit_rejects_g() -> unit
        val p = hex_digit()
        p(Input(as_bytes("g"), 0)) match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("hex_digit should reject 'g'")
            Failure(_, _) -> ()

    #test
    test_label_replaces_msg() -> unit
        val p = label(literal("foo"), "the foo keyword")
        p(Input(as_bytes("bar"), 0)) match
            Success(_, _) -> panic("should fail on 'bar'")
            Failure(m, _) ->
                if m != "expected the foo keyword" then
                    panic(s"unexpected msg: ${m}")

    #test
    test_label_passes_through_success() -> unit
        val p = label(literal("foo"), "the foo keyword")
        p(Input(as_bytes("foo"), 0)) match
            Success(v, _) -> if v != "foo" then panic("expected 'foo'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_skip_discards() -> unit
        val p = skip(literal("hello"))
        p(Input(as_bytes("hello!"), 0)) match
            Success(_, n) -> expect(n.offset, 5, "skip advanced past 'hello'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_pos_of_first_line() -> unit
        val inp = Input(as_bytes("hello"), 3)
        val (line, col) = pos_of(inp)
        expect(line, 1, "first line")
        expect(col, 4, "col 4 (1-indexed, after 'hel')")

    #test
    test_pos_of_after_newline() -> unit
        // "ab\ncd\nef" — offset 6 lands on 'e' on line 3
        val inp = Input(as_bytes("ab\ncd\nef"), 6)
        val (line, col) = pos_of(inp)
        expect(line, 3, "third line after two newlines")
        expect(col, 1, "col 1 at start of line")

    #test
    test_pos_of_at_start() -> unit
        val inp = Input(as_bytes("anything"), 0)
        val (line, col) = pos_of(inp)
        expect(line, 1, "line 1 at start")
        expect(col, 1, "col 1 at start")

    #test
    test_format_failure_single_line() -> unit
        val at = Input(as_bytes("xyz abc"), 4)
        val out = format_failure("expected number", at)
        // "line 1, col 5: expected number\nxyz abc\n    ^"
        if out != "line 1, col 5: expected number\nxyz abc\n    ^" then
            panic(s"unexpected format: ${out}")

    #test
    test_format_failure_multi_line() -> unit
        // Source has two lines; failure on the second.
        val at = Input(as_bytes("first\nsecond"), 9)
        val out = format_failure("oops", at)
        // line 2, col 4 → cursor at 'o' of "second"
        if out != "line 2, col 4: oops\nsecond\n   ^" then
            panic(s"unexpected format: ${out}")


Regex tests
-----------

    #test
    test_regex_digits_match() -> unit
        val p = regex("[0-9]+")
        p(Input(as_bytes("123abc"), 0)) match
            Success(v, n) ->
                if v != "123" then panic(s"expected '123', got '${v}'")
                expect(n.offset, 3, "advanced past digits")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_must_match_at_cursor() -> unit
        // regex anchors at cursor: 'abc123' must NOT match [0-9]+ at offset 0
        val p = regex("[0-9]+")
        p(Input(as_bytes("abc123"), 0)) match
            Success(v, _) -> panic(s"should not match anywhere; got '${v}'")
            Failure(_, _) -> ()

    #test
    test_regex_advances_correctly() -> unit
        // After regex consumes 'foo', a following literal must see 'bar'
        val p = regex("f[a-z]+") ~ literal("!")
        p(Input(as_bytes("foo!"), 0)) match
            Success(t, n) ->
                if t._0 != "foo" then panic(s"expected 'foo', got '${t._0}'")
                if t._1 != "!" then panic(s"expected '!', got '${t._1}'")
                expect(n.offset, 4, "advanced 4 bytes")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_leftmost_longest() -> unit
        // Within a match anchored at cursor, POSIX leftmost-longest applies
        val p = regex("a+")
        p(Input(as_bytes("aaaab"), 0)) match
            Success(v, n) ->
                if v != "aaaa" then panic(s"expected 'aaaa', got '${v}'")
                expect(n.offset, 4, "consumed all 4 a's")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_with_lex() -> unit
        // lex(regex(...)) is the canonical "skip ws, match, skip ws" idiom
        val p = lex(regex("[A-Za-z]+"))
        parse_all[string](p, "   hello   ") match
            Success(v, _) -> if v != "hello" then panic(s"expected 'hello', got '${v}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_alternation_anchored_first() -> unit
        // 'red|blue' against 'redball' — anchored match returns 'red'
        val p = regex("red|blue")
        p(Input(as_bytes("redball"), 0)) match
            Success(v, n) ->
                if v != "red" then panic(s"expected 'red', got '${v}'")
                expect(n.offset, 3, "advanced 3 bytes")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_empty_match_advances_zero() -> unit
        // '[0-9]*' matches empty at any position
        val p = regex("[0-9]*")
        p(Input(as_bytes("abc"), 0)) match
            Success(v, n) ->
                if v != "" then panic(s"expected '', got '${v}'")
                expect(n.offset, 0, "no advance for empty match")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_regex_in_grammar() -> unit
        // Toy use: 'word=value' where word and value are alphanumeric runs
        val word = regex("[a-z]+")
        val p = lex(word) ~ lex(literal("=")) ~ lex(word) ^^
            ((t: ((string, string), string)) -> s"${t._0._0}=${t._1}")
        parse_all[string](p, "  foo  =  bar  ") match
            Success(v, _) -> if v != "foo=bar" then panic(s"got '${v}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_r_alias() -> unit
        // `r("pat")` is the short alias — composes via the existing
        // Parser[string] operator overloads exactly like `regex(pat)`.
        val p = lex(r("[a-z]+")) ~ "=" ~ lex(r("[0-9]+")) ^^
            ((t: ((string, string), string)) -> s"${t._0._0}=${t._1}")
        parse_all[string](p, "x = 42") match
            Success(v, _) -> if v != "x=42" then panic(s"got '${v}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")

    #test
    test_string_r_extension() -> unit
        // The Scala-parity form: `"pattern".r` as a parameterless
        // extension method on string. Reads identically to
        // `RegexParsers`' `"\\d+".r ~ "=" ~ "[a-z]+".r`.
        val p = lex("[a-z]+".r) ~ "=" ~ lex("[0-9]+".r) ^^
            ((t: ((string, string), string)) -> s"${t._0._0}=${t._1}")
        parse_all[string](p, "abc = 7") match
            Success(v, _) -> if v != "abc=7" then panic(s"got '${v}'")
            Failure(m, _) -> panic(s"unexpected failure: ${m}")
